  "final_text": "I created the dashboard and the alert rule. The rule is enabled and its first run succeeded.\n\n**1. What the data holds:** The data view (`kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `timestamp`) contains web server access logs. The main fields are:\n- **Request:** `url`, `request`, `extension`, `agent`, `referer`\n- **Response:** `response` (the HTTP status code, stored as text), `bytes`\n- **Visitor:** `clientip`, `machine.os`, and location fields (`geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `geo.coordinates`)\n- **Other:** `host`, `tags`, `memory`, `phpmemory`, plus an `hour_of_day` field calculated from the timestamp\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-3992aa99 Web logs overview\"** (id `3ab5fe61-44d9-4578-8c45-a035a085c598`, default time range is the last 7 days). It has 8 panels:\n- Four headline numbers: total requests, unique visitors (distinct client IPs), average response size in bytes, and total bytes served.\n- Requests over time, stacked by response code.\n- 5xx error rate (%) over time.\n- Top 10 requested URLs.\n- Requests by operating system.\n\nThe last four charts use ES|QL queries, and I couldn't run those queries to check them because the Kibana query proxy is turned off. Kibana accepted the dashboard, but I haven't confirmed that those four charts display data. They're worth a quick look when you open it. The four headline numbers use the same setup as the examples in the API docs.\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-3992aa99 5xx spike\"** (id `961b6078-706a-4c7d-9538-4122f4206284`):\n- It's an Elasticsearch query rule that runs every minute on `kibana_sample_data_logs`.\n- It counts documents whose `response.keyword` starts with \"5\" (any 5xx code) in the last 5 minutes, and fires when the count is above 10.\n- No actions are attached, so it records alerts in Kibana but doesn't send notifications.",
